This study investigates the normalized ground states associated with the given biharmonic Choquard equation:
  \begin{align*}
  \begin{cases}
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda f(x,u)+\left(\rho\displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^N}\Phi_{\mu}(x,y)|u(y)|^2dy\right)f(x,u), & x\in\mathbb{R}^N, \\
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^N}u^2dy=c^2, & x\in\mathbb{R}^N,
  \end{array}
  \end{cases}
  \end{align*}
  where $\beta\geq 0$, $\lambda,\rho >0$, $0<\mu<\min\{2,N\}$, and $\Phi_{\mu}(x,y)=\frac{1}{\tau_{\mu}}K_{\mu}(\mu|x-y|)$, with $K_{\mu}$ being the modified Bessel function of the second kind. To establish the existence of normalized ground states for the equation, variational methods are employed, specifically minimizing the energy functional under the given $L^2$-constraint.

